  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints 27.06 and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded a one-digit fraction on the left with `rjust`, so \"12.5\" parsed as 12.05 (1205 cents) instead of 12.50. Two units at 1205 cents plus 8.25% tax gave 26.09. Padding on the right with `ljust` yields 1250 cents, and the tax rounds to the expected 27.06.\n\n**Change:** one line in `shop/money.py`, swapping `rjust` for `ljust` and dropping the now-redundant `or 0` fallback. Spot checks for \"12\", \"12.\", \"12.5\" and \"12.05\" all parse correctly.\n\n**Tests:** pytest is not installed, so I ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`. All 6 tests passed. None of the existing tests cover `to_cents` directly, so adding a regression test for one-decimal input would be a worthwhile follow-up.",
